BT /F1 11 Tf 72 596.00 Td (a tighter fit around the transition state. Cofactors are inorganic ions or organic coenzymes that) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 581.00 Td (some enzymes require for activity.) Tj ET
BT /F2 13 Tf 72 552.00 Td (2.2 Michaelis-Menten kinetics) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 530.00 Td (The Michaelis-Menten equation is v = Vmax [S] / \(Km + [S]\). Km is the substrate concentration) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 515.00 Td (at which the reaction runs at half of Vmax, so a low Km indicates high affinity for the substrate.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 500.00 Td (At very high substrate concentration, the enzyme is saturated and the rate approaches Vmax.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 477.00 Td (A Lineweaver-Burk plot graphs 1/v against 1/[S]. Its y-intercept is 1/Vmax and its x-intercept) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 462.00 Td (is -1/Km. The double reciprocal form makes it easy to compare inhibitors.) Tj ET
BT /F2 13 Tf 72 433.00 Td (2.3 Enzyme inhibition) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 411.00 Td (A competitive inhibitor binds the active site and competes with the substrate. It raises the) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 396.00 Td (apparent Km but does not change Vmax, because enough substrate can outcompete the inhibitor. A) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 381.00 Td (noncompetitive inhibitor binds an allosteric site with equal affinity for the free enzyme and the) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 366.00 Td (enzyme-substrate complex; it lowers Vmax and leaves Km unchanged.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 343.00 Td (An uncompetitive inhibitor binds only the enzyme-substrate complex and lowers both Km and Vmax.) Tj ET
